        <dataLineage>
                        			
            
            <prcStep>
                                				
                
                <stepDesc>In 1992, the Land Management Information Center (LMIC, now MnGeo) began a telecommunications project with the Minnesota Department of Commerce (the Department of Public Service at that time) as part of LMIC's Datanet online data service. MNDOC had exchange boundaries marked on the Minnesota Department of Transportation highway maps (1:126,720 or 1:63,360 scale). LMIC digitized the exchange, area code and LATA boundaries from these maps using EPPL7 version 2 software. A statewide EPPL7 vector file (.dgt) was created, then converted to ARC/INFO coverage format. The datum was NAD27. The last step in this project was the development of the first statewide telecommunications map. This map depicted exchange, area code and LATA boundaries. Exchanges were labeled and shaded by company. Over time, MNDOC and MnGeo have worked together in order to maintain this data by updating area code boundaries, ownership changes, exchange boundary changes, exchange company changes, and company name changes/corrections.

Process steps for 2013 to 2015 dataset:
The Minnesota Department of Commerce (MNDOC), Minnesota Telephone Alliance (MNTA), Minnesota Geospatial Information Office (MnGeo) and 100% of the telephone companies operating in Minnesota formed an ad hoc alliance in 2012/2013 to prepare these boundaries - encompassing all of Minnesota - for submission to the Federal Communications Commission, FCC Order DA 13-282.

To expedite the boundary review process prior to FCC submission, a web-based comment and mark-up application was created using Esri's ArcGIS Online (AGOL) product. In addition to map layers available in AGOL, the following digital base maps were provided to assist the companies:
- 2011 (or more recent) aerial photography served by MnGeo:  https://www.mngeo.state.mn.us/chouse/wms/geo_image_server.html
- 2013 municipal and county boundaries (CTUs):  https://gisdata.mn.gov/dataset/bdry-mn-city-township-unorg
- Public Land Survey (PLS) lines: https://gis.data.mn.gov/maps/dfa70fcba96e4d24ac73fef484b2da9c/about

Process:

1. MNTA coordinates boundary review activities with the companies. 

2. MnGeo conducts webinars to refresh the companies on how to use the AGOL application. It also provides a detailed help document to assist them with their review.

3. Telephone companies can review the boundaries and suggest corrections using a markup layer. Adjoining companies can agree to the proposed changes or suggest their own boundary.

4. Public Land Survey (PLS) quarter-quarter section data is used as a 'snapping' layer to assist the user in making accurate boundary changes.

5. MNDOC reviews the proposed boundary changes offered by the companies and makes corrections to the digital service territories after agreement on the boundary is reached with the companies. Historical, printed maps of the boundaries housed at MNDOC serve as guides to resolve any differences in opinion.

6. GIS data updates:
a. Once MNDOC makes all the agreed upon corrections to the GIS data, MnGeo checks the topology for gaps and overlaps. All topological errors are corrected using several layers as guides: the markup layer, existing exchange boundaries and quarter-quarter section lines.

b. Companies are given a final opportunity to review the data before the submission to the FCC.

Subsequent updates have followed a similar process.</stepDesc>
                                			
            
            </prcStep>
                        			
            
